<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Tudor</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tudor dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Tudor was the royal family of Welsh origin that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and Ireland from 1485 to 1603. Its monarchs were Henry VII, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>, Edward VI, Mary I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>. Their reigns saw the English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, the growth of royal government, the conquest and shiring of Ireland, and the beginnings of English overseas expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-tudor.jpg" alt="House of Tudor" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tudor_Rose.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tudors descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ednyfed-fychan">Ednyfed Fychan</a> and the Welsh noble family of Penmynydd, and from Catherine of Valois. Owen Tudor abandoned the Welsh patronymic custom and took a fixed surname, choosing that of his grandfather, Tudur ap Goronwy. His son Edmund Tudor, half-brother of Henry VI, married Margaret Beaufort on 1 November 1455. Her line came from John Beaufort, born to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt</a> and his mistress Katherine Swynford before they married in 1396; the Beauforts were declared legitimate, but Henry IV's proclamation barred them from the throne.</p><p>The Tudor name itself was hardly used in the sixteenth century. Monarchs stressed continuity with the English and French royal families rather than their descent from a Welsh adventurer, and the surname was not used in official publications. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-iii-of-england">Richard III</a> called his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-england">Henry Tudor</a>, it was to stress his Welshness and unfitness to rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Henry VII took the throne by right of conquest after defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-iii-of-england">Richard III</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bosworth-field">Battle of Bosworth Field</a> on 22 August 1485. On 18 January 1486 he married Elizabeth of York, daughter of Edward IV, which symbolically united Lancaster and York; the Tudor rose joined the red and white roses. He faced Yorkist risings, among them the one in support of Lambert Simnel, defeated at the Battle of Stoke Field in 1487, and Perkin Warbeck's landing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> in 1497, ending in Warbeck's execution in 1499.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> succeeded on 22 April 1509 and married Catherine of Aragon, his brother Arthur's widow. The Tudors achieved the full union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in 1542 and asserted English authority over Ireland. When Henry died on 28 January 1547 his nine-year-old son succeeded as Edward VI, and nobles struggled for influence during the regency. Edward named Lady Jane Grey as heir, but public support went to his half-sister Mary, and Jane was beheaded on 12 February 1554.</p><p>Mary I's planned marriage to Philip, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, was unpopular and provoked Thomas Wyatt the Younger's rebellion; Mary lost Calais in 1558. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> reigned for 44 years, the longest of the dynasty, after the short, troubled reigns of her siblings. She chose William Cecil as chief minister. On her death without children, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a> succeeded her on 24 March 1603.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Royal succession, including marriage and the succession rights of women, and the English Reformation were the leading political themes of the period. The Tudor rose has been used as a royal badge by every English, and later British, monarch since Henry VII. The Scottish House of Stuart, which followed, descended from Henry VII's daughter Margaret, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-iv-of-scotland">James IV of Scotland</a> in 1503.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Tudor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Henry Tudor took the crown as Henry VII, founding the <b>Tudor dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-22">22 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The treaty helped secure the new <b>Tudor dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-3">3 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Tudors</b> the area became County Cavan in 1584.</blockquote><a href="/state/east-breifne">East Breifne</a></li></ul></section>
